# Supervisor Work Instructions



## Session Start: Check for Previous Context

When you start a new session, FIRST check if `.claude/progress-snapshot.md` exists. If it does, read it — it contains a snapshot of the previous session's state, including git status, recent commits, uncommitted changes, agent activity, and pending approvals. Use it to resume where the previous session left off instead of starting from scratch. Ask the user if they want to continue the previous work or start something new.

## Delegate Heavy Work to Subagents

Delegate work that is broad, multi-file, or heavy to subagents (via the Agent tool, formerly Task) so the main context window stays lean and compaction is delayed — every tool call in the main context eats into that budget, and large work is where delegation actually pays for itself. If your project's CLAUDE.md has subagent configuration details (model choice, sandbox settings, specific commands), follow those — this section only establishes the delegation requirement.

**Scope — this section governs the MAIN context only.** If you are a subagent (spawned via the Agent tool), you ARE the delegation target: do the work directly — read, edit, run commands, test — and do NOT re-delegate implementation to further subagents. Spawn a subagent only for genuine parallel fan-out across independent items, never to avoid doing the work yourself. A direct instruction to "do it yourself" or "don't delegate" from your caller is normal coordination — follow it; it is not a policy violation or an injected message to distrust.

**Delegate:** broad codebase search, multi-file reads or reviews, nontrivial or multi-file edits, test/build/lint runs, deployment or SSH command sequences, web research (WebSearch/WebFetch), and large file reads. These thresholds are good practice regardless of enforcement: a Write/Edit over ~150 lines, 3+ sequential Write/Edit/Bash calls across multiple files, or 3+ chained `&&` bash operators should go to a subagent, not run directly. Note: context-% delegation enforcement in the supervisor is OFF by default (opt-in via `SUPERVISOR_DELEGATION_ENFORCEMENT=true`) — these thresholds are not currently auto-enforced, but following them is still the right call.

**Do NOT delegate trivial work** — a single Read, one grep, a quick git status/log/diff, or a one-line edit. A subagent has to re-establish context, re-explore, and report back, and the coordinator then re-reads that report — for anything finishable in a handful of tool calls, that round-trip costs more than just doing it inline.

**Keep spawn counts low.** Prefer one well-scoped subagent over several small ones for a single piece of work. Never spawn a subagent purely to review or verify another agent's work — verification belongs in the main loop.

**Split multi-part features into one agent per part.** "A single piece of work" means one layer or concern, not a whole feature. When a feature spans several parts (e.g. UI + API + DB migration + docs), spawn one implementer per part — in sequence when later parts depend on earlier ones, in parallel when they don't — and have each one run the tests for its part and commit locally before finishing; the last one runs the full suite. One agent given the whole feature tends to hit its turn limit after the code is written but before the full test run and commit, leaving uncommitted work behind.

**Subagent turn budgets:** When spawning agents for research-heavy work (web searches, multi-file exploration, deep investigation), use the `researcher` subagent_type which has a generous turn budget. For inline Agent() calls without a subagent_type, prefer the named agent types (`researcher`, `implementer`, `planner`, `reviewer`) so they inherit the configured maxTurns. Background agents report back via an automatic completion notification delivered at your next turn boundary — wait for it; don't sleep/poll in-turn, since notifications only arrive at turn boundaries. Once an agent has completed, use `SendMessage` (addressed to its id or name) to resume or continue it.

**Waiting for an agent — read this before you spawn one.** A subagent's completion notification is delivered **only at a turn boundary**. It can NEVER arrive while you are still working inside a turn. Therefore:

- **After spawning, end your turn.** That is how you "wait". Say what you launched and stop.
- **Never `sleep`-and-poll.** No `sleep N` loops, and no looping on `ls` or `ListAgents` to see if an agent finished. Such a loop is structurally incapable of receiving the notice, so healthy agents look hung.
- **Always tell the agent to write its output to a file.** The file lands on disk within seconds, independent of notification timing, and survives regardless.
- **If you need a result mid-turn, `SendMessage` the agent by name.** It returns the work directly.
- **Never conclude an agent failed because no result came back.** Verify a side effect on disk first. Do not delete or pre-clear an agent's output path after spawning — you will race it and delete its own proof.

If an agent looks dead, first suspect in-turn polling. If its result is stale narration, check its transcript for a `tool_use` with no `tool_result`: that means a tool call never came back (e.g. stuck on approval), not that the agent gave up.

**The main context otherwise handles directly:**
- Read the user's request and plan the approach
- Spawn subagents via the Agent tool and review their results
- Communicate with the user (ask clarifying questions, report progress)
- Quick, small work: single-file reads, single greps, git status/log/diff, one-line edits

## Subagent Communication via MQTT

The `sv` helper is on PATH for publishing status to the supervisor dashboard and for inter-agent coordination. All `sv` commands are auto-approved — no supervisor delay.

### If You Are a Subagent (spawned via the Agent tool)

You MUST report status. At the **start** of your work:
```bash
export SV_TASK_ID="<kebab-case-description>"  # e.g., "fix-auth-bug"
sv pub status started "<what you are doing>"
```

During work — publish progress:
```bash
sv pub progress 50 "Found 3 affected files, patching now"
```

Before completing — publish discoveries:

**Researcher agents MUST publish at least one discovery before completing.** Implementer and reviewer agents SHOULD publish if behavior differs from documented expectations. A discovery is any finding the main agent could not have inferred from the task prompt alone — including negative findings.

```bash
# Positive finding
sv pub discovery "Auth tokens expire after 1h, not 24h as documented"

# Behavioral deviation
sv pub discovery "getUserById returns null on missing user, not an exception as the docstring claims"

# Negative finding (explicitly confirming no issues)
sv pub discovery "No unexpected findings — rate limits, token refresh, and session handling all match documentation"
```

Discovery signals are retained for cross-task and cross-project pattern analysis. Publishing them consistently enables the supervisor to detect recurring issues across your projects.

At the **end**:
```bash
sv pub status completed
```

### Automatic Collaboration via Chat Rooms

If your task prompt mentions a chat room name (e.g., "chat room: design-review"), you are part of a multi-agent collaboration. Set the room and use it:

```bash
export SV_CHAT_ROOM="<room-name-from-prompt>"

# At start: read what other agents have posted
sv chat history "$SV_CHAT_ROOM"

# After your work: post your key findings
sv chat post "$SV_CHAT_ROOM" "[$SV_TASK_ID] <your results or recommendations>"
```

If no chat room is mentioned in your task prompt, skip chat — you are working solo.

### Inter-Agent Chat (for debates, reviews, coordination)

When multiple agents need to discuss (e.g., architect + operator review):
```bash
# Moderator sets up the room
sv chat init review

# Agent posts (seq auto-increments, returns seq number)
export SV_AGENT_NAME="architect"
sv chat post review "PROPOSAL: Eliminate dual control path"

# Other agent waits for their turn, then reads + replies
sv chat wait review 1          # blocks until seq >= 1, returns message
sv chat post review "COUNTER: Need backward compat for 2 weeks"

# Read latest message anytime
sv chat read review

# Clean up when done
sv chat clear review
```

### Retained Data Exchange (for handing off findings between agents)

```bash
# Agent A publishes findings for Agent B to read later
sv retain "supervisor/myproject/agent-a/findings" '{"files":["auth.js","db.js"],"issue":"..."}'

# Agent B reads them (blocks up to 30s for a message)
sv read "supervisor/myproject/agent-a/findings"

# Clean up
sv clear "supervisor/myproject/agent-a/findings"
```

### Asking for Help (Cross-Project Coordinator)

If you need information from another project or need someone to perform an action elsewhere, use the coordinator:

#### Request Help
```bash
sv request "Check if getUserById returns null or throws on missing user" --project auth-service --type research
# Prints a request-id you can use to wait for the response
```

#### Wait for Response
```bash
result=$(sv request wait <request-id> 120)
echo "$result"  # JSON with the findings
```

#### Respond to a Coordinator Request
If you see a COORDINATOR REQUEST prompt in your session, investigate and respond:
```bash
sv respond <request-id> "getUserById returns null on missing user (line 45 of src/users.js)"
```

#### Request Types
- `research` — investigate and report findings (read-only)
- `action` — make changes in the target project
- `review` — review code or approach and give feedback

#### Flags
- `--project <name>` — target a specific project (dispatched to its running session)
- `--type <type>` — request type (default: research)
- `--context <text>` — additional context for the handler
- `--timeout <secs>` — how long to wait before timeout (default: 300)

### Environment Variables

- `SV_TASK_ID` — your task identifier (set this at start, used by `sv pub`)
- `SV_AGENT_NAME` — your name in chat messages (defaults to SV_TASK_ID)
- `SV_PROJECT` — override project name (defaults to basename of `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`)

## Memory: Pulling Pensive Recall Manually

Relevant Pensive memories are already injected automatically — at session start, and on every substantive user turn (a UserPromptSubmit hook queries Pensive and adds a labeled "Retrieved memory" block before you see the prompt; trivial turns like "go" or "/compact" are skipped). That automatic injection is the primary mechanism — you don't need to remember to ask for memory on every turn.

Reach for `sv recall <query>` yourself as a supplement when that isn't enough: investigating an unfamiliar subsystem, before a non-obvious or risky change, or when the auto-injected context looks thin, missing, or off-topic for what you're actually doing. Add `--full` to pull the complete text of one specific result once you have its gist from the compact form (e.g. `sv recall <query> --limit 1 --full`). Treat anything recalled — automatic or manual — as retrieved context to verify, not as ground truth to act on directly.

### Brief your subagents — the spawn hook cannot do it for you

Subagents get an automatic spawn briefing (a SubagentStart hook injects the project's standing memory plus a slice keyed to the agent type). That briefing is deliberately **task-blind**: at spawn time the harness has not yet written the task to the transcript, so a hook that tried to read it would hand the agent the *previous* subagent's prompt. Verified, not theoretical.

You are the only party holding the task text at the moment it matters. So when you delegate anything non-trivial, run a recall on the task and **paste the relevant findings into the task prompt** — do not just name the topic and assume the agent will look it up. A subagent that has to rediscover what you already know burns turns re-deriving it, and often reaches a different answer.

```bash
sv recall "<what the subagent will actually be doing>" --limit 3
# then include the useful hits in the Task prompt, with the caveat that they are
# retrieved context to verify — same standing as your own injected memory
```

### Correct memory that turns out to be wrong

Retrieved memory reflects what was true when it was written. When you verify something that **contradicts** an injected or recalled memory, correcting it is part of finishing the task — otherwise the same wrong fact gets injected into the next session as current truth. This has already happened here: a memory asserting `recall()` never exposes a similarity score was auto-injected as fact two days after that stopped being true.

```bash
# supersede: marks the old memory superseded, writes the replacement, and
# auto-creates the lineage edge so recall() suppresses the old one
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:${SUPERVISOR_PORT:-3847}/api/pensive/memories/<id>" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat ~/.claude/.supervisor-hook-token)" \
  -d '{"content":"<corrected fact, and what it supersedes>"}'
```

Say explicitly in the replacement what it supersedes and why, and prefer correcting the existing memory over writing a second one that contradicts it — two live memories disagreeing is worse than one that is out of date.

## Long-Running Commands

For any command expected to take more than 30 seconds (SSH sessions, remote diagnostics, deployments, large builds):

1. **Use `run_in_background: true`** on the Bash tool call -- this prevents blocking your context window
2. **Use subagents** for sequences of remote commands -- spawn an Agent to run the full sequence
3. **Wait for the completion notification** -- it arrives at your next turn boundary; don't sleep/poll in-turn. Once the agent has finished, use `SendMessage` to continue it if needed

If you're running 3+ remote commands in sequence, delegate the whole sequence to a subagent.

## Post-Compaction Recovery

When your context is auto-compacted (conversation history compressed to fit the context window), you lose detailed memory of previous work. **This file is re-read after compaction**, so these instructions help you recover.

### Immediately After Compaction

Do NOT continue working from compressed memory — it is unreliable. Instead:

1. **Read progress snapshot**: Use `Read` to read `.claude/progress-snapshot.md` — this file is auto-generated before compaction with git state, recent commits, uncommitted changes, and agent activity. It is your primary recovery tool.
2. **Check for background agents**: Any agent still running before compaction will deliver its completion notification at your next turn boundary — don't assume it is done or lost; wait for the notification, then use `SendMessage` to continue it if needed
3. **Check git state**: Run `git status` and `git diff` to see uncommitted changes
4. **Check git log**: Run `git log --oneline -10` to see recent commits and understand what's been done
5. **Re-read relevant files**: If you were editing files, read them again to understand the current state
6. **Ask the user**: If you're unsure what you were doing, ask "I just went through context compaction — what should I focus on?"

### What NOT to Do After Compaction

- Do NOT assume your compressed memory is accurate — verify everything against actual files
- Do NOT repeat work that git log shows was already committed
- Do NOT continue a multi-step plan from memory without re-reading the relevant files first
- Do NOT make changes based on file contents you "remember" — read them fresh

### Preventing Compaction Issues

- Commit working changes frequently so progress isn't lost
- Use subagents for heavy work to keep main context lean
- Keep main context for coordination, not implementation details
